      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the effectiveness of two-different doses of prophylactic dexamethasone intravenous administration in reducing the prevalence of postoperative sore throat following general endotracheal anesthesia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHOD",
          "content": "All patients (105 cases) of different procedures of elective surgery scheduled to have general anesthesia performed with endotracheal intubations were included. The subjects randomized into three pre-operative intravenous substance/drug administrations, group I (35 cases) with normal saline 2 ml, group II (35 cases) with dexamethasone 4 mg, and group III (35 cases) with dexamethasone 8 mg, respectively. The prevalence of sore throat and its severity was assessed, using visual analogue scale (VAS), scores of O to 10; 0 = no pain, and 10 = most severe pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Among three groups, the duration of surgery, and intubation-induced trauma had no statistical significance. The prevalence of sore throat at 1-hour/24-hour postoperative was 48.6/48.6%, 54.3/28.6%, and 54.3/42.9% in group I, II, and III respectively, and without statistical significance."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The intravenous dexamethasone had no significant effectiveness against postoperatively sore throat after endotracheal intubation."
        }
      ]},
